{
 "_what": "The one key table for every screen; a screen that adds its own key table repeats it. The ONE keymap: every key chord Trillion's pages answer, as DATA (src/workspace/keymap.mjs reads it, keymap-runtime.ts listens, keymap-settings.tsx lets the person re-record each one under Settings, Shortcuts). Another feature adds its keys by adding rows here and registering the verb its row names (registerVerb in keymap-runtime.ts); it never listens for keys on its own. A row is `{ chord, verb, scope, words }`: `chord` is modifiers then a key joined by `+` (`mod` is Cmd on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere; `ctrl`, `alt`, `shift` and `meta` are themselves; the key is a name from `keys`) or null when the row starts with no key; `verb` names what runs; `scope` names a row of `scopes` where it works; `words` is what the person reads. A global row may carry `host` naming something only the Mac app itself does (`toggle-app` shows or hides Trillion); it then has no `verb` on the page to run. A row may also carry `with` (what its verb is handed, for example the screen `open-screen` opens) and `id` (its name when two rows share one verb; the verb is its name otherwise). A person's own keys are kept as their account record `workspace-preference:` + `preference` (`{ <row name>: chord | null }`, null being a key taken away). `scopes`: a screen scope may be `within` another (a narrower place inside it: an event focused inside the calendar, the task tray beside it); while both match the narrower one's row is heard first. Kind `global` is registered with the computer itself, so it works while another app is in front (the Mac app registers it; it needs a modifier); kind `app` works on every Trillion page; kind `screen` works only while the page holds an element matching its `selector`. `keys` maps each key name to the KeyboardEvent.code it is (`code`), the Mac virtual key code the host registers (`mac`) and how it is shown (`shown`). `plain_keys` decides keys pressed alone (no modifier): they never run while the person is typing in a field, and run only when no menu, list, tab or slider holds the keyboard (`held`); the same goes for a key with only Shift or Option, which can type a character. `host` is how the Mac app hears the `global` rows: the page tells them on message channel `channel` (macos/App/global-keys.json says the same) and the app calls `window.<page_entry>.pressed(<row name>)` when one is pressed. `reserved` are chords the system keeps for itself and cannot be taken. `words` is every sentence the Shortcuts page and the host say.",
